  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Saturday through Monday: A slow moving weather system will bring\n  prolonged, heavy rainfall, focused along the divide and in\n  Glacier Park region. \n\n- Significant river rises from widespread rain and snow melt,\n  leading to the potential minor flooding. \n\n- Snow levels fall near 6,500-7,500 feet tonight into Sunday\n  morning, bringing wet snow across the high backcountry.\n\nA slow moving low pressure system will track northward across\nWyoming into central Montana by this afternoon and evening. High-\nresolution model guidance suggests widespread rain will develop by\nlate morning to early afternoon along the divide in southwest\nMontana, with rain spreading across western Montana through the\nevening to overnight hours. This low will tap into abnormally high\nmoisture, with PWAT values running 200% of normal along the\ndivide. A west-northwest flow will strengthen overnight into\nSunday, favoring higher precipitation totals across the Glacier\nPark region, the Mission and Swan Mountains and the Continental\nDivide in southwest Montana, with 1 to 3 inches of precipitation\nare expected. The highest amounts will focus across the Glacier\npark region, where localized amounts up to 4 inches can't be ruled\nout through Monday morning. The highest valley rain totals will\ncenter along eastern portions of the Flathead and Mission \nvalleys, where 1.00-2.00 inches are expected. Confidence in \nvalley rain totals begins to decrease along and west of the US-93 \ncorridor. Recent model trends have shifted the upper level low \nfurther east, suggesting lower confidence for higher rain totals \nacross these locations. \n\nGiven recent rainfall from showers and thunderstorms the last\nseveral days, many areas are experiencing saturated soils. Given\nthe soil conditions, and widespread rain combined with ongoing \nhigh-elevation snowmelt, confidence is increasing for sharp rises\non area waterways. The Clark Fork River above Missoula, the \nFlathead River at Columbia Falls, and Swan River near Big Fork \nare currently forecast to reach minor flood stage late Sunday or \nearly Monday. Minor flooding and water ponding on roads are also a\nconcern for low-lying and urban areas, particularly within \nFlathead and Lake Counties. Residents in flood-prone locations \nshould clear storm drains and gutters ahead of time. In addition \nto water impacts, steep canyon terrain will face an elevated risk \nof rockfalls and mudslides. Meanwhile, sharp cooling aloft will \ncause snow levels to drop to 6,000 to 7,000 feet along the \nContinental Divide by Sunday morning, bringing raw, winter-like \nconditions and hazardous travel to backcountry users.\n\nOnce the storm system exits, weather models favor a brief transition\nback to a high-pressure ridge by the middle of the week. By\nThursday and Friday next week, forecast confidence begins to\ndecrease with ensemble model guidance suggesting the potential for\nanother round of Pacific systems and widespread rain, while other\nsolutions suggest warm and drier conditions.",
